{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "olsson-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B016",
        "laureates": [
          "David Huang",
          "Eric A. Swanson",
          "James G. Fujimoto"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention of optical coherence tomography for noninvasive depth-resolved optical imaging"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B043",
    "B035"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B016 identifies a bounded original instrumental invention: a low-coherence interferometric method made reliable, noninvasive, micrometre-scale depth-resolved imaging of living tissue possible. Its physical performance and its durable deployment have been repeatedly validated over decades. The proposed trio remains a coherent compact attribution to the foundational demonstration and development. This is a physics invention that opened a new observational capability; clinical consequence is confirming evidence of robustness, not the basis of the citation.",
    "case_against": "The decisive remaining issue is priority: archival review must separate the OCT invention from antecedent low-coherence interferometry and from later clinical translation, and must test whether these three names exclusively capture the foundational work. The direct biomedical setting also requires an especially precise citation so that the award recognizes the original optical measurement invention rather than its subsequent applications.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll prefers B043 because the magic-wavelength lattice-clock architecture is independently reproducible, mature, and compactly attributed to Katori and Ye, while noting the need to distinguish it from the wider optical-clock lineage.",
        "response": "I agree that B043 is an exceptionally strong alternative, particularly because independent reproduction is compelling evidence that an instrument has become a durable means of measurement. But the unresolved lineage boundary is material to exact credit. For B016, the proposed achievement can be stated as a specific original imaging invention, with a direct experimental transition from optical principle to a new way of observing internal structure; pending priority review, I judge that boundary more readily delimited."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that B043 should rest on the demonstrated magic-wavelength lattice as a controllable many-atom system, rather than on a future redefinition of the second.",
        "response": "That is the correct restraint and strengthens B043 as an alternative. The same standard favors OCT: its case does not depend on prospective clinical extensions or commercial diffusion, but on the already demonstrated physical ability to obtain depth-resolved images noninvasively. I continue to give priority to the invention whose observational function and founding experimental claim can be stated most directly."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson accepts that OCT is a physical imaging invention but cautions that its biomedical character could suggest recognition outside physics.",
        "response": "The caution is useful. I would therefore avoid a citation centered on diagnosis, medicine, or impact. Low-coherence interferometric depth sectioning is itself a physics-based measurement invention: it created experimentally accessible information that ordinary optical imaging could not provide. That instrumental advance, rather than the field in which it is used, is the proposed subject of the prize."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review confirms that Huang, Swanson, and Fujimoto fully and exclusively represent the foundational OCT invention after antecedent low-coherence interferometry is separated from it.",
      "Whether the final citation can draw a sufficiently exact boundary between the original depth-resolved optical imaging invention and subsequent clinical implementation."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
